  "realtimeStudio.demo.defaultTranscript": "Host A|Let us review today's goals: confirm realtime transcription, structure graph linking, and report export are stable.|sequential\nProduct Manager B|I care most about transcript readability, especially whether different speakers are easy to distinguish.|structural\nResearcher C|I will add a third perspective to see whether color recognition stays clear as three people alternate.|structural\nHost A|Good. I will add context first: this test includes three speakers taking turns.|sequential\nProduct Manager B|If the colors are distinct, we can use them in the demo environment and record it in the release checklist.|feedback_loop\nResearcher C|We should also confirm mixed long and short sentences remain readable.|structural",
  "realtimeStudio.demo.scriptedTurns": "Product Manager|Let's walk through the customer support assistant release. I want the feedback intake, satisfaction board, and knowledge updates to connect as one loop.|scope\nSupport Lead|The night shift raised two issues: after three turns the system is unsure about handoff, and agents cannot tell when knowledge edits become active.|input\nEngineer|I'll put the feedback intake on the graph first. It comes from tickets, live chats, and agent notes, not just one page click.|input\nData Analyst|For the satisfaction board, I would split the metric into resolution rate, sentiment recovery, and human handoff watch. A single score hides the cause.|quality\nProduct Manager|Resolution rate should use the final result of the latest conversation, not count every clarification as an unresolved issue.|quality\nSupport Lead|Sentiment recovery matters too. Many customers begin frustrated and later accept the answer, and that shift tells us whether the reply helped.|quality\nDesigner|The human handoff prompt should not be a large modal. It should be visible beside the conversation while keeping the agent's current context.|quality\nEngineer|The first graph stage can stop at intake and quality metrics. We won't reveal resilience, knowledge, or release checks yet.|stage_one\nProduct Manager|Next is multi-turn resilience. Real customers come back a few minutes later and add that the order was from another store.|resilience\nSupport Lead|Exactly. Agents worry when the system mixes two issues, especially when a customer jumps from refund to inventory.|resilience\nEngineer|We keep a fifteen minute session memory, but every turn re-evaluates topic_tag and can trigger a context reset.|resilience\nData Analyst|topic_tag is not just a category. It affects satisfaction attribution, so topic switches inside one conversation must be counted separately.|resilience\nDesigner|Conversation group color can show whether the user is continuing the same issue or starting a new issue group.|resilience\nProduct Manager|If the user clearly says this is another question, the system should lightly confirm the context switch and stop using the previous answer.|resilience\nEngineer|The context reset path keeps a summary, but it does not push the old issue back into the model prompt, so the answer is not polluted.|resilience\nQA|I'll write four cases for this: return after timeout, explicit topic switch, implicit topic switch, and return after handoff.|qa_resilience\nProduct Manager|The third block is knowledge updates. Operations need new campaign rules to show up quickly, but saving each edit must not trigger a rebuild.|knowledge\nOperations|On Fridays we often edit a dozen rules in a row. If each save rebuilds, the queue and server disk both get hit hard.|knowledge\nEngineer|So edits go into a delta queue. Nearby edits within two minutes are merged into one low-priority rebuild job.|knowledge\nDevOps|The deploy script also needs a disk preflight, cleanup after upload, and rollback if extraction fails, so half-written folders do not remain.|knowledge\nProduct Manager|For users, we only need a lightweight notice: knowledge updated, the next answer will use the new version. Do not interrupt typing.|knowledge\nDesigner|The notice can sit above the input, collapse after three seconds, and leave one version-change marker in history.|knowledge\nQA|After publishing knowledge, I'll run regression for new-rule hit, old-rule miss, and no repeated notices.|qa_knowledge\nProduct Manager|Finally, release verification. The graph should show QA cases, deployment guardrails, and the review view without digging through meeting notes.|release\nQA|If the graph can generate the checklist, it should cover resolution rate, context reset, merged rebuild, and handoff prompt at minimum.|release\nSupport Lead|Agent training also needs one page that explains when to trust the system hint and when to take over manually.|release\nDevOps|I'll add disk preflight, cleanup before upload, and rollback on failure to prevent short bursts of writes from filling the server.|release\nData Analyst|After launch we watch three metrics: resolution rate lift, handoff reduction, and abnormal spikes in knowledge rebuild duration.|release\nEngineer|Demo mode should play four pre-generated stages instead of calling the model live, so recordings and reviews stay stable.|demo\nProduct Manager|Good. The final frame should show intake, quality metrics, resilience, knowledge updates, and release verification connected in one result graph.|wrap",
